  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enabling Safe Return Through Rehabilitating Apartments</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Over a year into the broad military offensive against Ukraine, there are 3.6 million people still displaced. This number has declined significantly since January 2023, with many people seeking to return to their areas of habitual residence. Among Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs), 74 per cent intend to return to their place of habitual residence, and five per cent intend to do so within the next two weeks. However, 17 per cent of IDPs indicate damage to their homes as a main barrier to return. They continue to live in collective centres or other temporary solutions as they lack the financial means and/or technical capacity to repair their homes and return to their communities. 
 
The International Organization for Migration (IOM) is supporting the safe return of Ukrainian IDPs to Irpin, Kyivska oblast, through the rehabilitation of apartment homes and community development initiatives. These community development initiatives, based on consultations with community members, include the provision of solar street lighting and a playground. These interventions contribute towards creating conditions for the safe and dignified return of populations displaced as a result of the war.</narrative>
  </description>
